WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:01.590
YURI GRINSHTEYN: You've seen
how to set up monitoring

00:00:01.590 --> 00:00:03.330
and learning for GKE services.

00:00:03.330 --> 00:00:04.990
But what do you do
when an alert fires?

00:00:04.990 --> 00:00:06.870
How can you tell
what's actually wrong?

00:00:06.870 --> 00:00:08.828
Today I'm going to show
you how to troubleshoot

00:00:08.828 --> 00:00:09.900
services running on GKE.

00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:11.810
This is The Stack Doctor.

00:00:11.810 --> 00:00:15.100
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:21.160
One of my favorite new features
in cloud or operations for GKE

00:00:21.160 --> 00:00:22.240
is the Alerts Timeline.

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:24.550
It lets you quickly see if
any of the infrastructure

00:00:24.550 --> 00:00:27.040
or workloads in your
workspace are having an issue.

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:29.140
If an incident is triggered,
you can mouse over it

00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:31.210
in the timeline to get
more information about it.

00:00:31.210 --> 00:00:33.310
For example, here's an
infrastructure incident.

00:00:33.310 --> 00:00:34.690
It's telling you
when it started,

00:00:34.690 --> 00:00:36.368
which resource is
having an issue,

00:00:36.368 --> 00:00:38.410
and which alerting policy
triggered the incident.

00:00:38.410 --> 00:00:40.660
From here, you can click the
View Incident link to get

00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:43.040
the full incident details.

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:44.780
The Details screen
shows you exactly what

00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:47.030
happened to trigger the
incident, which resources were

00:00:47.030 --> 00:00:48.572
involved, and how
the networks you're

00:00:48.572 --> 00:00:50.300
tracking in the
alerting policy changed

00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:51.590
to exceed the threshold.

00:00:51.590 --> 00:00:53.690
From here you can click
on the View Logs link

00:00:53.690 --> 00:00:57.470
at the bottom to figure
out exactly what happened.

00:00:57.470 --> 00:00:58.970
That takes you to
the Logs Explorer,

00:00:58.970 --> 00:01:00.260
where the query
is already written

00:01:00.260 --> 00:01:02.468
for you to pull the logs
with just the resources that

00:01:02.468 --> 00:01:03.500
triggered the incident.

00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:05.600
The log entries show you
exactly what happened

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:06.740
that caused the incident.

00:01:06.740 --> 00:01:08.930
In this example, the
containers are crashing.

00:01:08.930 --> 00:01:11.023
Now, figuring out what
to do to fix the problem

00:01:11.023 --> 00:01:13.190
may not be easy, but getting
to this point where you

00:01:13.190 --> 00:01:16.130
know exactly what's wrong is.

00:01:16.130 --> 00:01:17.950
As you may recall from
an earlier episode,

00:01:17.950 --> 00:01:19.540
it's also possible
to integrate traces

00:01:19.540 --> 00:01:21.820
and logs using OpenTelemetry
or other methods.

00:01:21.820 --> 00:01:24.297
If you've done that, you'll
see this icon next to the log

00:01:24.297 --> 00:01:26.380
entries, letting you know
that an associated trace

00:01:26.380 --> 00:01:28.310
is available.

00:01:28.310 --> 00:01:30.723
Click on that icon, and
click View Trace Details.

00:01:30.723 --> 00:01:32.890
This will take you to the
trace for the request that

00:01:32.890 --> 00:01:33.712
generated that log.

00:01:33.712 --> 00:01:35.920
You can then try to determine
whether the problem has

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:38.470
to do with the downstream
service or a dependency.

00:01:38.470 --> 00:01:39.280
And there you are.

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:40.405
Here are the trace details.

00:01:40.405 --> 00:01:42.880
If you want more information,
click the View and Trace link

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:45.280
to go to the trace list and
see the full list of tracers,

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:47.060
including this one.

00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:48.320
Thanks for joining me today.

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:49.820
We looked at the
new Alerts Timeline

00:01:49.820 --> 00:01:51.938
in the GKE Monitoring
dashboard, and learned

00:01:51.938 --> 00:01:53.480
how to use it to
get from an incident

00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:55.670
all the way to the logs
and associated traces

00:01:55.670 --> 00:01:57.530
we need to troubleshoot
our service.

00:01:57.530 --> 00:02:00.320
I hope these new features help
you run your services on GKE

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:01.670
more reliably.

00:02:01.670 --> 00:02:03.720
Thanks for watching, and
come back soon for more.

00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:04.790
This is the Stack Doctor.

00:02:04.790 --> 00:02:06.410
Stay healthy out there.

00:02:06.410 --> 00:02:10.060
[MUSIC PLAYING]

